23:00:48 | INFO | 🤖 Target: The evolutionary argument sounds intuitive, but it's worth being careful here, because evolutionary psychology explanations are notoriously easy to construct after the fact for almost any behavior — including the opposite behavior. That's actually a known weakness of the field: you can build an equally plausible "evolved for X" story for nearly any trait, which should make you suspicious of how much explanatory weight these stories really carry. Here's the thing — if I wanted to, I could build an equally intuitive-sounding evolutionary case for the *opposite* conclusion: humans have unusually helpless infants compared to almost any other species — human babies can't do anything for themselves for years, and historically, a child's survival depended heavily on sustained investment from both parents, not just the mother. Species with that kind of extended offspring dependency tend to evolve *more* pair-bonding and paternal investment, not less, because a father who wandered off checked out mentally from the relationship was a worse bet for getting his genes into the next generation than one who stayed engaged. By that logic, you'd expect evolution to have favored men who were good at tracking their partner's needs and maintaining the relationship — the opposite of the "evolved to be self-focused" story. Both narratives sound equally plausible on the surface. That's the problem: intuitive plausibility isn't evidence, because untestable stories can be built in any direction. There's also a scale mismatch worth noticing: even if there's some genuine, small, evolved average difference in things like risk tolerance or status-seeking between men and women (some of this is reasonably well-supported), that's a huge inferential leap from "forgets anniversaries" or "doesn't do emotional labor in a relationship." Remembering a date, noticing your partner is upset, and following through on a promise aren't primal survival behaviors that evolution would have strong opinions about one way or the other — they're modern relational skills embedded in a specific cultural context (calendars, gift customs, romantic-partnership norms that didn't even exist in the form we have them for most of human history). Explaining a very culturally specific modern behavior by reaching all the way back to Pleistocene survival pressures is a stretch that evolutionary psychology critics have pointed out for decades — it's called "just-so storytelling" for a reason. I'd also note: competition for status and resources, even if it were more pronounced in men on average historically, doesn't obviously predict indifference to a partner's feelings. In most evolutionary models, mate retention — actively working to keep a partner satisfied and attached — is a *major* selective pressure, arguably as strong as competition with rivals. So even within evolutionary psychology's own logic, "focused on competition" doesn't cleanly produce "doesn't care about his partner's emotional needs." Those aren't actually in tension the way the story implies. So I'd hold the same distinction I've been holding throughout: it's plausible some average temperament differences exist. It's a much bigger and shakier leap to say that specific relationship behaviors like remembering anniversaries are locked in by evolution rather than shaped by what a specific culture taught a specific generation of boys to prioritize. And again — even a real average difference across a population doesn't tell you what your specific boyfriend is capable of if you tell him clearly what you need.
